   "source": [
    "The standard deviation columns have NaN values only when there aren't at least 2 prior purchases."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c093fabc-ab0c-4ec2-9742-975f702a1820",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For customers that have 0 prior purchases it's normal for them to not have days_since_previous_purchase , prior_mean_gap_days and prior_std_gap_days since they have never bought anything before.\n",
    "For customers with 1 prior purchase, they do have days_since_previous_purchase but since they only purchased once, they don't have a prior_mean_gap_days because there's no second purchase to measure the gap days. This also means there won't be a prior_std_gap_days.\n",
    "For customers with 2 prior purchases, there is only 1 gap, so we can calculate the prior_mean_gap_days but not the prior_std_gap_days since we can't calcluate a variantion from a single number.\n",
    "\n",
    "So how do we fill these missing values?\n",
    "We can fill prior_std_gap_days with 0 for these cases because customers with 0; 1 or 2 purchases have no variation in their gaps.\n",
    "For the customers that purchases 1 time, we can set prior_mean_gap_days eaqual to the days_since_previous_purchase. And for customers with 0 purchases we set the prior_mean_gap_days to 0."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 40,
   "id": "cb067324-5701-4e22-8937-a91cb10d458e",
